[
    [
        {
            "type": "title",
            "content": {
                "title_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "1 Introduction"
                    }
                ],
                "level": 2
            },
            "bbox": [
                127,
                81,
                342,
                103
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "title",
            "content": {
                "title_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "1.1 What is Machine Learning"
                    }
                ],
                "level": 2
            },
            "bbox": [
                125,
                127,
                481,
                148
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Machine learning is a subfield of computer science that is concerned with building algorithms which, to be useful, rely on a collection of examples of some phenomenon. These examples can come from nature, be handcrafted by humans or generated by another algorithm."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                124,
                166,
                872,
                222
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Machine learning can also be defined as the process of solving a practical problem by 1) gathering a dataset, and 2) algorithmically building a statistical model based on that dataset. That statistical model is assumed to be used somehow to solve the practical problem."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                124,
                229,
                872,
                283
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "To save keystrokes, I use the terms “learning” and “machine learning” interchangeably."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                124,
                292,
                835,
                310
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "title",
            "content": {
                "title_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "1.2 Types of Learning"
                    }
                ],
                "level": 2
            },
            "bbox": [
                125,
                340,
                388,
                363
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Learning can be supervised, semi-supervised, unsupervised and reinforcement."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                124,
                379,
                762,
                399
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "title",
            "content": {
                "title_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "1.2.1 Supervised Learning"
                    }
                ],
                "level": 2
            },
            "bbox": [
                125,
                427,
                388,
                445
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "In supervised learning<sup>1</sup>, the dataset is the collection of labeled examples "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\{ ( \\mathbf { x } _ { i } , y _ { i } ) \\} _ { i = 1 } ^ { N }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Each element "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\mathbf { x } _ { i }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "among N is called a feature vector. A feature vector is a vector in which each dimension "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "j = 1 , \\dots , D"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "contains a value that describes the example somehow. That value is called a feature and is denoted as "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "x ^ { ( j ) }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": ". For instance, if each example x in our collection represents a person, then the first feature, "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "x ^ { ( 1 ) }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": ", could contain height in cm, the second feature, "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "x ^ { ( 2 ) }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": ", could contain weight in kg, "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "x ^ { ( 3 ) }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "could contain gender, and so on. For all examples in the dataset, the feature at position j in the feature vector always contains the same kind of information. It means that if "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "x _ { i } ^ { ( 2 ) }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "contains weight in kg in some example "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\mathbf { x } _ { i } ."
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "then "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "x _ { k } ^ { ( 2 ) }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "will also contain weight in kg in every example "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\mathbf { x } _ { k } , k = 1 , \\ldots , N"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": ". The label "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "y _ { i }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "can be either an element belonging to a finite set of classes "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\{ 1 , 2 , \\ldots , C \\}"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": ", or a real number, or a more complex structure, like a vector, a matrix, a tree, or a graph. Unless otherwise stated, in this book "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "y _ { i }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "is either one of a finite set of classes or a real number. You can see a class as a category to which an example belongs. For instance, if your examples are email messages and your problem is spam detection, then you have two classes spam, not_spam ."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                124,
                463,
                872,
                723
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "The goal of a supervised learning algorithm is to use the dataset to produce a model that takes a feature vector x as input and outputs information that allows deducing the label for this feature vector. For instance, the model created using the dataset of people could take as input a feature vector describing a person and output a probability that the person has cancer."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                124,
                729,
                872,
                821
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "page_number",
            "content": {
                "page_number_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "3"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                855,
                903,
                870,
                918
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "page_footnote",
            "content": {
                "page_footnote_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "<sup>1</sup>In this book, if a term is in bold, that means that this term can be found in the index at the end of the book."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                124,
                825,
                872,
                855
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "page_footer",
            "content": {
                "page_footer_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Andriy Burkov The Hundred-Page Machine Learning Book - Draft"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                125,
                903,
                707,
                921
            ]
        }
    ],
    [
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "is 20,000-dimensional). The algorithm puts all feature vectors on an imaginary 20,000- dimensional plot and draws an imaginary 20,000-dimensional line (a hyperplane) that separates examples with positive labels from examples with negative labels. In machine learning, the boundary separating the examples of di\u0000erent classes is called the decision boundary."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                124,
                85,
                874,
                159
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "The equation of the hyperplane is given by two parameters, a real-valued vector w of the same dimensionality as our input feature vector x, and a real number b like this:"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                122,
                166,
                872,
                204
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "equation_interline",
            "content": {
                "math_content": "\\mathbf {w x} - b = 0,",
                "math_type": "latex",
                "image_source": {
                    "path": "images/6c206393ae423b549f45e618fb211cd2f7b98db2b286877ec88ba4a0cd8b7bd1.jpg"
                }
            },
            "bbox": [
                444,
                229,
                550,
                246
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "where the expression wx means "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "w ^ { ( 1 ) } x ^ { ( 1 ) } + w ^ { ( 2 ) } x ^ { ( 2 ) } + . . . + w ^ { ( D ) } x ^ { ( D ) }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": ", and D is the number of dimensions of the feature vector x."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                122,
                262,
                870,
                301
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "(If some equations aren’t clear to you right now, in Chapter 2 we revisit the math and statistical concepts necessary to understand them. For the moment, try to get an intuition of what’s happening here. It all becomes more clear after you read the next chapter.)"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                122,
                309,
                872,
                364
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Now, the predicted label for some input feature vector x is given like this:"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                124,
                372,
                733,
                391
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "equation_interline",
            "content": {
                "math_content": "y = \\mathrm{sign} (\\mathbf {w x} - b),",
                "math_type": "latex",
                "image_source": {
                    "path": "images/ef7350b4ef54e17662256bb5b1ab641e270907b535d6795eaacd159742b0fcbe.jpg"
                }
            },
            "bbox": [
                420,
                418,
                574,
                436
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "where sign is a mathematical operator that takes any value as input and returns +1 if the input is a positive number or 1 if the input is a negative number."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                122,
                453,
                870,
                490
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "The goal of the learning algorithm — SVM in this case — is to leverage the dataset and find the optimal values "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\mathbf { w } ^ { * }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "and "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "b ^ { * }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "for parameters w and b. Once the learning algorithm identifies these optimal values, the model "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "f ( \\mathbf { x } )"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "is then defined as:"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                124,
                498,
                872,
                554
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "equation_interline",
            "content": {
                "math_content": "f (\\mathbf {x}) = \\mathrm{sign} (\\mathbf {w} ^ {*} \\mathbf {x} - b ^ {*})",
                "math_type": "latex",
                "image_source": {
                    "path": "images/ed383e95bc8c1ece3a8153b12c7d50add562fbf6489077497beb7a6f60c38e84.jpg"
                }
            },
            "bbox": [
                401,
                579,
                594,
                599
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Therefore, to predict whether an email message is spam or not spam using an SVM model, you have to take a text of the message, convert it into a feature vector, then multiply this vector by "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\mathbf { w } ^ { * }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": ", subtract "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "b ^ { * }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "and take the sign of the result. This will give us the prediction (+1 means “spam”, 1 means “not_spam”)."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                122,
                615,
                872,
                687
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Now, how does the machine find "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\mathbf { w } ^ { * }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "and "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "b ^ { \\ast } ?"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "It solves an optimization problem. Machines are good at optimizing functions under constraints."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                124,
                695,
                870,
                732
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "So what are the constraints we want to satisfy here? First of all, we want the model to predict the labels of our 10,000 examples correctly. Remember that each example i = 1, . . . , 10000 is given by a pair "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\left( \\mathbf { x } _ { i } , y _ { i } \\right)"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": ", where "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\mathbf { x } _ { i }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "is the feature vector of example i and "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "y _ { i }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "is its label that takes values either 1 or +1. So the constraints are naturally:"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                124,
                741,
                872,
                813
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\mathbf { w x } _ { i } - b \\geq 1 { \\mathrm { ~ i f ~ } } y _ { i } = + 1"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": ", and"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                150,
                821,
                405,
                839
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\mathbf { w x } _ { i } - b \\leq - 1 { \\mathrm { ~ i f ~ } } y _ { i } = - 1"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                151,
                840,
                377,
                857
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "page_footer",
            "content": {
                "page_footer_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Andriy Burkov The Hundred-Page Machine Learning Book - Draft"
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                125,
                902,
                707,
                921
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "page_number",
            "content": {
                "page_number_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "6"
                    }
